INFO 07-17 13:06:46 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-6bb8647c76824443905934d5dbec4f5a-0: prompt: 'Let\'s answer a question directly and concisely.\nQuestion: Context: Edward John Noble, the owner of Life Savers candy, drugstore chain Rexall and New York City radio station WMCA, purchased the network for $8 million. Due to FCC ownership rules, the transaction, which was to include the purchase of three RCA stations by Noble, would require him to resell his station with the FCC\'s approval. The Commission authorized the transaction on October 12, 1943. Soon afterward, the Blue Network was purchased by the new company Noble founded, the American Broadcasting System. Noble subsequently acquired the rights to the "American Broadcasting Company" name from George B. Storer in 1944; its parent company adopted the corporate name American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. Woods retained his position as president and CEO of ABC until December 1949, and was subsequently promoted to vice-chairman of the board before leaving ABC altogether on June 30, 1951.\nImplications: 1. Noble owned a few industries, like life savers candy and a drugstore chain, which he sold.\n2. Noble bought the American Broadcasting Company along with WMCA and other radio stations for $8 million.\n3. At first, it seemed like Noble could keep all the stations and expand his business, but he was required to sell WMCA to the FCC.\n4. ABC became part of a new company named the American Broadcasting System.\n5. In 1944, the company also bought the right to use the name "American Broadcasting Company."\n6. Woods, the president and CEO of ABC, was promoted to vice-chairman of the board after Noble made more changes to the company\'s ownership. Then, he left the company in 1951.\nTopic: American Broadcasting Company\nWhat candy company did Edward John Noble own?\nAnswer:\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.