INFO 07-17 13:08:09 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-cb7bd5c9167a454292a966b298c06026-0: prompt: 'Let\'s answer a question directly and concisely.\nQuestion: Context: One theory suggests the name stems from a palatalised version of the Mongolian and Turkic word tenggis, meaning "ocean", "oceanic" or "wide-spreading". (Lake Baikal and ocean were called tenggis by the Mongols. However, it seems that if they had meant to call Genghis tenggis they could have said, and written, "Tenggis Khan", which they did not.) Zhèng (Chinese: 正) meaning "right", "just", or "true", would have received the Mongolian adjectival modifier -s, creating "Jenggis", which in medieval romanization would be written "Genghis". It is likely that the 13th century Mongolian pronunciation would have closely matched "Chinggis".\nImplications: - Genghis Khan\'s name may have a connection to the Mongolian and Turkic word for "ocean", "oceanic", or "wide-spreading".\n- The name Genghis may not have referred to a specific geographical location, such as Lake Baikal or the ocean, but rather a more abstract concept.\n- The term "Jenggis" may have been a palatalised version of the Mongolian and Turkic word for "ocean", "oceanic", or "wide-spreading".\n- The use of the suffix -s in the Mongolian language could have given rise to the name "Genghis".\n- The use of the character "Zhèng" in the name Genghis Khan could have carried the meaning of "right", "just", or "true", which was likely intended by the Mongolian speakers.\n- The use of the Mongolian language in referring to Genghis Khan may have influenced the Romanization of his name, with "Chinggis" being a possible transliteration.\n- The Mongolian pronunciation of Genghis Khan\'s name may have been similar to the pronunciation of "Chinggis".\nTopic: Genghis Khan\nWhich Mongolian word meaning wide-spreading may have contributed to the appellation Genghis?\nAnswer:\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_pe