INFO 07-17 13:12:32 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-f1dbc039d6f04434b0fadd6268ce4b73-0: prompt: 'Let\'s answer a question directly and concisely.\nQuestion: Context: But bounding the computation time above by some concrete function f(n) often yields complexity classes that depend on the chosen machine model. For instance, the language {xx | x is any binary string} can be solved in linear time on a multi-tape Turing machine, but necessarily requires quadratic time in the model of single-tape Turing machines. If we allow polynomial variations in running time, Cobham-Edmonds thesis states that "the time complexities in any two reasonable and general models of computation are polynomially related" (Goldreich 2008, Chapter 1.2). This forms the basis for the complexity class P, which is the set of decision problems solvable by a deterministic Turing machine within polynomial time. The corresponding set of function problems is FP.\nImplications: 1. The computational complexity of a problem depends on the chosen machine model.\n2. The time complexities of problems in different machine models are polynomially related.\n3. P is the set of decision problems solvable by a deterministic Turing machine within polynomial time.\n4. FP is the corresponding set of function problems for P.\n5. Cobham-Edmonds thesis suggests that the choice of machine model does not significantly affect the complexity of a problem.\n6. The relationship between time complexities in different machine models is fundamental to computational complexity theory.\n7. The polynomial relationship between time complexities in different machine models is useful for understanding the relationship between problems in different complexity classes.\n8. The fact that P and FP are sets of problems solvable by a deterministic Turing machine within polynomial time implies that these problems are computationally tractable.\n9. Cobham-Edmonds thesis suggests that polynomial-time algorithms are a good measure of computational efficiency.\n10. The polynomial-time complexity of problems in different machine models implies that there is a trade-off between the computational efficiency of different machine models.\nTopic: Computational complexity theory\nConcrete bounding of computation time frequently produces complexity classes contingent upon what?\nAnswer:\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[151643], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=64, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [10061, 594, 4226, 264, 3405, 5961, 323, 3529, 285, 974, 624, 14582, 25, 9608, 25, 1988, 30618, 279, 34447, 882, 3403, 553, 1045, 14175, 729, 282, 1445, 8, 3545, 35408, 23094, 6846, 429, 6761, 389, 279, 11882, 5662, 1614, 13, 1752, 2867, 11, 279, 4128, 314, 4146, 760, 856, 374, 894, 7868, 914, 92, 646, 387, 27956, 304, 13482, 882, 389, 264, 7299, 2385, 2027, 94430, 5662, 11, 714, 14312, 7460, 79151, 882, 304, 279, 1614, 315, 3175, 2385, 2027, 94430, 12645, 13, 1416, 582, 2138, 47311, 26244, 304, 4303, 882, 11, 45951, 5604, 12, 2715, 54620, 17394, 5302, 429, 330, 1782, 882, 83040, 304, 894, 1378, 13276, 323, 4586, 4119, 315, 34447, 525, 47311, 398, 5435, 1, 320, 25434, 40334, 220, 17, 15, 15, 23, 11, 15585, 220, 16, 13, 17, 568, 1096, 7586, 279, 8037, 369, 279, 23094, 536, 393, 11, 892, 374, 279, 738, 315, 5480, 5322, 2048, 23760, 553, 264, 72349, 94430, 5662, 2878, 47311, 882, 13, 576, 12159, 738, 315, 729, 5322, 374, 33551, 624, 25216, 10709, 25, 220, 16, 13, 576, 54480, 23094, 315, 264, 3491, 13798, 389, 279, 11882, 5662, 1614, 624, 17, 13, 576, 882, 83040, 315, 5322, 304, 2155, 5662, 4119, 525, 47311, 398, 5435, 624, 18, 13, 393, 374, 279, 738, 315, 5480, 5322