bined land and sea analysis were reduced somewhat in the new analysis (Figure 2.1).\n\nThe revision of the ocean record affects the overall global mean temperature record by about 0.03°C. It increases the mean ocean warming over the period 1950–1998 from +0.04°C ±0.06°C in Jones et al. (1994) to +0.06°C ±0.06°C in this analysis. These changes do not affect significantly the overall conclusion that ocean warming is consistent with atmospheric and continental warming.\n\nThere is a poleward shift in the land warming with 87% of the warming in the Northern Hemisphere occurring north of 30°N latitude and just 8% of warming in the Southern Hemisphere south of 30°S latitude. These values are substantially different from earlier results of 64% and 14%, respectively, for the period 1951 to 1996 (Hansen et al., 2000a).\n\nThe amplified warming in the Arctic in the revised analysis reflects the effect of the reduced boundary layer adjustment to the surface temperature, which arises from modifications to the calculation of the temperature inversion based on modifications to the procedure used to homogenize radiosonde observations for change of station (see Chapter 3, Figure 3.15).\n\nChapter 2 also addresses the large cooling in the Southern Hemisphere in the mid-1970s reported in the early IPCC Third Assessment Report (Hansen et al., 1998; Murphy et al., 2000). The warmest year in this period was 1979 and, since that time, the warming has continued without interruption (see Figure 2.6). In fact, annual mean temperatures in the Southern Hemisphere have reached levels equivalent to those attained in the Northern Hemisphere during the 1940s.\nTopic: Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change\nWhen did Barton and Whitfield demand climate research records?\nAnswer:\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[151643], bad_words=[], include_stop