INFO 07-17 13:16:55 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-600b911d40444e418a7d8a9e101a2682-0: prompt: "Let's answer a question directly and concisely.\nQuestion: Context: In contrast, during wake periods differentiated effector cells, such as cytotoxic natural killer cells and CTLs (cytotoxic T lymphocytes), peak in order to elicit an effective response against any intruding pathogens. As well during awake active times, anti-inflammatory molecules, such as cortisol and catecholamines, peak. There are two theories as to why the pro-inflammatory state is reserved for sleep time. First, inflammation would cause serious cognitive and physical impairments if it were to occur during wake times. Second, inflammation may occur during sleep times due to the presence of melatonin. Inflammation causes a great deal of oxidative stress and the presence of melatonin during sleep times could actively counteract free radical production during this time.\nImplications: 1. Differentiated effector cells, such as natural killer cells and cytotoxic T lymphocytes, are more active during awake periods to effectively combat intruding pathogens.\n2. During wake periods, anti-inflammatory molecules like cortisol and catecholamines also peak to modulate the immune response.\n3. There are two proposed reasons for reserving a pro-inflammatory state for sleep times:\n   a. Inflammation could lead to significant cognitive and physical impairments if it were to occur during wake times.\n   b. The presence of melatonin in sleep times might actively counteract free radical production during the inflammatory response.\nTopic: Immune system\nWhat are examples of differentiated effector cells that peak during wake periods?\nAnswer:\n", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[151643], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=64, m