INFO 07-17 13:18:51 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-125811c45d09493cbe36d10ed8c53236-0: prompt: 'Let\'s answer a question directly and concisely.\nQuestion: Context: The phrase "Hiding behind (or \'watching from behind\') the sofa" entered British pop culture, signifying in humour the stereotypical early-series behaviour of children who wanted to avoid seeing frightening parts of a television programme while remaining in the room to watch the remainder of it. The phrase retains this association with Doctor Who, to the point that in 1991 the Museum of the Moving Image in London named their exhibition celebrating the programme "Behind the Sofa". The electronic theme music too was perceived as eerie, novel, and frightening, at the time. A 2012 article placed this childhood juxtaposition of fear and thrill "at the center of many people\'s relationship with the show", and a 2011 online vote at Digital Spy deemed the series the "scariest TV show of all time".\nImplications: 1. The Doctor Who phrase "Hiding behind the sofa" is a pop culture reference that represents the behavior of children watching scary parts of a TV show.\n2. The theme music of Doctor Who was initially perceived as eerie and frightening, making children feel scared and thrilled at the same time.\n3. Doctor Who has a deep-rooted association with childhood fear and thrill, with many people\'s relationship to the show revolving around these emotions.\n4. The "Behind the Sofa" exhibition at the Museum of the Moving Image was specifically created to celebrate Doctor Who.\n5. The use of a theme music and the "Hiding behind the sofa" phrase indicate that Doctor Who had a significant impact on pop culture and nostalgia.\nTopic: Doctor Who\nWhat popular phrase is associated with the Doctor Who series?\nAnswer:\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[151643], bad_wo