INFO 07-17 13:20:42 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-f189a53505ab4e01a1af989d3d476ac0-0: prompt: "Let's answer a question directly and concisely.\nQuestion: Context: By the 6th century, the Rhine was within the borders of Francia. In the 9th, it formed part of the border between Middle and Western Francia, but in the 10th century, it was fully within the Holy Roman Empire, flowing through Swabia, Franconia and Lower Lorraine. The mouths of the Rhine, in the county of Holland, fell to the Burgundian Netherlands in the 15th century; Holland remained contentious territory throughout the European wars of religion and the eventual collapse of the Holy Roman Empire, when the length of the Rhine fell to the First French Empire and its client states. The Alsace on the left banks of the Upper Rhine was sold to Burgundy by Archduke Sigismund of Austria in 1469 and eventually fell to France in the Thirty Years' War. The numerous historic castles in Rhineland-Palatinate attest to the importance of the river as a commercial route.\nImplications: 1. The Rhine has historical significance dating back to the 6th century as it was part of Francia's borders.\n2. In the 9th century, it formed part of the border between Middle and Western Francia.\n3. By the 10th century, the Rhine was fully within the Holy Roman Empire, passing through various territories such as Swabia, Franconia, and Lower Lorraine.\n4. The mouths of the Rhine, located in the county of Holland, became part of the Burgundian Netherlands in the 15th century.\n5. Holland remained under Burgundian control throughout various European wars of religion and the eventual decline of the Holy Roman Empire.\n6. In the 18th and 19th centuries, the Rhine fell to the First French Empire and its client states.\n7. The Alsace region, situated on the left banks of the Upper Rhine, was sold to Burgundy by Archduke Sigismund of Austria in 1469 and later ceded to France in the Thirty Years' War.\n8. The strategic importance of the Rhine as a commercial route is reflected by the numerous historic castles found in Rhineland-Palatinate.\nTopic: Rhine\nWhen did the Rhine become borders with Francia?\nAnswer:\n", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[151643], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=64, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [10061, 594, 4226, 264, 3405, 5961, 323, 3529, 285, 974, 624, 14582, 25, 9608, 25, 3216, 279, 220, 21, 339, 9294, 11, 279, 17968, 482, 572, 2878, 279, 23806, 315, 9694, 685, 13, 758, 279, 220, 24, 339, 11, 432, 14122, 949, 315, 279, 3886, 1948, 12592, 323, 10867, 9694, 685, 11, 714, 304, 279, 220, 16, 15, 339, 9294, 11, 432, 572, 7225, 2878, 279, 18710, 12751, 20448, 11, 35512, 1526, 4492, 370, 685, 11, 30825, 443, 685, 323, 27536, 444, 90022, 13, 576, 64509, 315, 279, 17968, 482, 11, 304, 279, 13867, 315, 37815, 11, 11052, 311, 279, 40009, 1241, 1103, 25662, 304, 279, 220, 16, 20, 339, 9294, 26, 37815, 14616, 66500, 17971, 6814, 279, 7513, 24936, 315, 13587, 323, 279, 41735, 18179, 315, 279, 18710, 12751, 20448, 11, 979, 279, 3084, 315, 279, 17968, 482, 11052, 311, 279, 5512, 8585, 20448, 323, 1181, 2943, 5302, 13, 576, 44462, 578, 389, 279, 2115, 13959, 315, 279, 30614, 17968, 482, 572, 6088, 311, 40009, 59185, 553, 9494, 1054, 440, 41560, 2142, 1241, 315, 34898, 304, 220, 16, 19, 21, 24, 323, 9583, 11052, 311, 9625, 304, 279, 66620, 22315, 6, 5004, 13, 576, 12114, 18035, 6311, 642, 304, 17968, 258, 18123, 9299, 278, 266, 3277, 86917, 311, 279, 12650, 315, 279, 14796, 438, 264, 8353, 6021, 624, 25216, 10709, 25, 220, 16, 13, 576, 17968, 482, 702, 13656, 25361, 4924, 1182, 311, 279, 220, 21, 339, 9294, 438, 432, 572, 949, 315, 