INFO 07-17 13:22:55 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-f4d772fc7afd40df8ffae534c5066252-0: prompt: "Let's answer a question directly and concisely.\nQuestion: Context: When some species, including Bathyctena chuni, Euplokamis stationis and Eurhamphaea vexilligera, are disturbed, they produce secretions (ink) that luminesce at much the same wavelengths as their bodies. Juveniles will luminesce more brightly in relation to their body size than adults, whose luminescence is diffused over their bodies. Detailed statistical investigation has not suggested the function of ctenophores' bioluminescence nor produced any correlation between its exact color and any aspect of the animals' environments, such as depth or whether they live in coastal or mid-ocean waters.\nImplications: 1. Certain species of ctenophores, when disturbed, release secretions that emit light at the same wavelengths as their bodies.\n2. Luminescence in juvenile ctenophores is brighter in comparison to their body size in comparison to adults.\n3. The luminescence of ctenophores' bodies is diffused, reducing its brightness in adults.\n4. Detailed statistical investigations have not revealed the function of ctenophores' bioluminescence.\n5. There is no clear correlation between ctenophores' bioluminescence color and their environments, such as depth or habitat type (coastal or mid-ocean).\nTopic: Ctenophora\nWhat do bathyctena chuni, euplokamis stationis and eurhamphaea vexilligera have in common?\nAnswer:\n", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[151643], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=64, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [10061, 594, 4226, 264, 3405, 59