INFO 07-17 13:25:22 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-00137d519fdc4b17ad73675648feaf05-0: prompt: "Let's answer a question directly and concisely.\nQuestion: Context: Almost all the rocks show evidence of impact process effects. Many samples appear to be pitted with micrometeoroid impact craters, which is never seen on Earth rocks, due to the thick atmosphere. Many show signs of being subjected to high pressure shock waves that are generated during impact events. Some of the returned samples are of impact melt (materials melted near an impact crater.) All samples returned from the Moon are highly brecciated as a result of being subjected to multiple impact events.\nImplications: 1. The samples are not from Earth due to the lack of atmosphere.\n2. The rocks have been subjected to shock waves and high pressure, indicating exposure to impact events.\n3. Some samples are impact melts, indicating that they were formed near impact craters.\n4. The samples are highly brecciated, indicating multiple impact events.\n\nLet's read the following passage and produce a list of implications derived directly or indirectly from the content.\n\nPassage:\nAll of the primary mission objectives had been accomplished by the Apollo 11 mission, successfully establishing the crew's capability to perform a safe landing on the Moon, recover useful scientific data, and conduct useful scientific operations. The missions that followed, Apollo 12, 14, 15, 16, and 17, expanded on the research and skills started by Apollo 11, adding to lunar sample collections and conducting more complex operations and experiments.\n\nImplications:\n1. Apollo 11 successfully landed on the Moon.\n2. The crew was able to recover valuable scientific data.\n3. The crew was able to conduct useful scientific operations.\n4. The missions that followed built upon the success of Apollo 11, increasing the quantity and quality of scientific data collected.\n\nLet's read the following passage and produce a list of implications derived directly or indirectly from the content.\n\nPassage:\nOrbit altitude\n\nPassage:\nThe orbit of an object around Earth is determined primarily by the initial state vector of the launch vehicle that sent the object into orbit. Gravity and aerodynamic drag from the Earth's atmosphere affect the spacecraft's orbit over time.\n\nImplications:\n1. The initial state vector of the launch vehicle is a critical factor in determining the orbit of an object around the Earth.\n2. Gravity and aerodynamic drag from the Earth's atmosphere affect the spacecraft's orbit over time, which can lead to changes in the orbit.\n\nLet's read the following passage and produce a list of implications derived directly or indirectly from the content.\n\nPassage:\nAn army of typewriters in front of a flag. Typewriters produce types, which are bound into typographic books. The pages of such a typographic book often have sections of text in different sizes, types, colors, or shapes.\n\nImplications:\n1. Typewriters are used to produce types.\n2. Types are bound into typographic books.\n3. Typographic books can have pages with sections of text in different sizes, types, colors, or shapes.\nTopic: Apollo program\nAs the moon has no atmosphere, what is prevalant in many moon rock samples brought back from Apollo missions?\nAnswer:\n", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[151643], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=64, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [10061, 594, 4226, 264, 3405, 5961, 323, 3529, 285, 974, 624, 14582, 25, 9608, 25, 34303, 678, 279, 23035, 1473, 5904, 315, 5421, 1882, 6239, 13, 8999, 10469, 4994, 311, 387, 281, 3762, 448, 19197,