INFO 07-17 13:31:45 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-5ae9d13158c6493497462407856230e3-1: prompt: 'Let\'s answer a question directly and concisely.\nQuestion: Context: But even after NASA reached internal agreement, it was far from smooth sailing. Kennedy\'s science advisor Jerome Wiesner, who had expressed his opposition to manned spaceflight to Kennedy before the President took office, and had opposed the decision to land men on the Moon, hired Golovin, who had left NASA, to chair his own "Space Vehicle Panel", ostensibly to monitor, but actually to second-guess NASA\'s decisions on the Saturn V launch vehicle and LOR by forcing Shea, Seamans, and even Webb to defend themselves, delaying its formal announcement to the press on July 11, 1962, and forcing Webb to still hedge the decision as "tentative".\nImplications: 1. The Apollo program faced opposition and challenges even after reaching internal agreement.\n2. Jerome Wiesner, a science advisor to President Kennedy, actively opposed manned spaceflight and the Moon landing decision.\n3. Wiesner hired Golovin, who had previously left NASA, to chair the "Space Vehicle Panel" for the purpose of second-guessing NASA\'s decisions.\n4. The actions of Wiesner and Golovin led to the delay of the formal announcement of NASA\'s decision on the Saturn V launch vehicle and LOR.\n5. The opposition from Wiesner and his "Space Vehicle Panel" forced NASA officials, including Shea, Seamans, and Webb, to defend their decisions and face challenges.\n6. The delay in the announcement and the opposition made Webb hesitant to make a firm decision on the LOR program.\n7. The Apollo program\'s development was not smooth sailing, and it faced numerous obstacles and opposition during its initial stages.\n8. There were political and ideological disagreements within the administration and advisors regarding the direction of the space program.\n9. These disagreements hindered the progress of NASA\'s decision-making process and resulted in significant delays and bureaucratic issues.\n10. Despite the challenges, NASA eventually reached an agreement and made decisions, but the opposition and resistance it faced from certain individuals and advisors undermined its efforts.\nTopic: Apollo program\nWho did Wiesner hire to monitor and second guess NASA\'s decisions?\nAnswer:\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[151643], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=64, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [10061, 594, 4226, 264, 3405, 5961, 323, 3529, 285, 974, 624, 14582, 25, 9608, 25, 1988, 1496, 1283, 21431, 8643, 5306, 9128, 11, 432, 572, 3041, 504, 10876, 50029, 13, 23651, 594, 8038, 36613, 69774, 467, 550, 1194, 11, 879, 1030, 13302, 806, 13758, 311, 88325, 3550, 38390, 311, 23651, 1573, 279, 4795, 3867, 5163, 11, 323, 1030, 16087, 279, 5480, 311, 4268, 2953, 389, 279, 17330, 11, 21446, 54579, 859, 258, 11, 879, 1030, 2115, 21431, 11, 311, 10496, 806, 1828, 330, 9914, 24707, 18946, 497, 80494, 311, 8718, 11, 714, 3520, 311, 2086, 2371, 33301, 21431, 594, 11181, 389, 279, 49153, 647, 7050, 7310, 323, 444, 868, 553, 24913, 84968, 11, 77966, 596, 11, 323, 1496, 54388, 311, 10505, 5577, 11, 79330, 1181, 15908, 17043, 311, 279, 3493, 389, 5768, 220, 16, 16, 11, 220, 16, 24, 21, 17, 11, 323, 24913, 54388, 311, 2058, 41666, 279, 5480, 438, 330, 72368, 1388, 22956, 25216, 10709, 25, 220, 16, 13, 576, 35150, 2025, 16601, 13758, 323, 11513, 1496, 1283, 18739, 5306, 9128, 624, 17, 13, 69774, 467, 550, 1194, 11, 264, 8038, 36613, 311, 4795, 23651, 11, 22040, 16087, 88325, 3550, 38390, 323, 279, 17330, 20327, 5480, 624, 18, 13, 467, 550, 1194, 21446, 54579, 859, 258, 11, 879, 103