INFO 07-17 13:33:11 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-af73b0ce812046bc836a0aada347b32a-0: prompt: "Let's answer a question directly and concisely.\nQuestion: Context: Governor Robert Dinwiddie of Virginia was an investor in the Ohio Company, which stood to lose money if the French held their claim. To counter the French military presence in Ohio, in October 1753 Dinwiddie ordered the 21-year-old Major George Washington (whose brother was another Ohio Company investor) of the Virginia Regiment to warn the French to leave Virginia territory. Washington left with a small party, picking up along the way Jacob Van Braam as an interpreter; Christopher Gist, a company surveyor working in the area; and a few Mingo led by Tanaghrisson. On December 12, Washington and his men reached Fort Le Boeuf.\nImplications: 1. The Ohio Company had a vested interest in maintaining control of the Ohio territory and was not pleased with the French military presence in the area.\n2. Governor Robert Dinwiddie's decision to send Major George Washington to warn the French may have been motivated by his financial ties to the Ohio Company.\n3. The French presence in Ohio was seen as a threat to Virginia's claim over the region.\n4. Major George Washington, being 21 years old and an investor in the Ohio Company, may have been assigned this mission in part to gain valuable experience.\n5. The party sent by Dinwiddie to Fort Le Boeuf included a translator, Christopher Gist, who was working in the area as a surveyor for the Ohio Company.\n6. The Mingo, led by Tanaghrisson, were likely to be allies of the French and potentially opposed to Virginia's interests in the Ohio territory.\nTopic: French and Indian War\nGovernon Robert Dinwiddie had an investment in what significan company?\nAnswer:\n", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[151643], bad_words=[], in