INFO 07-17 13:35:57 [logger.py:43] Received request cmpl-4f0907a3ca5a458eb3eae21f4f391ecf-0: prompt: 'Let\'s answer a question directly and concisely.\nQuestion: Context: After an unmanned LM test flight AS-206, a crew would fly the first Block II CSM and LM in a dual mission known as AS-207/208, or AS-278 (each spacecraft would be launched on a separate Saturn IB.) The Block II crew positions were titled Commander (CDR) Command Module Pilot (CMP) and Lunar Module Pilot (LMP). The astronauts would begin wearing a new Apollo spacesuit, designed to accommodate lunar extravehicular activity (EVA). The traditional visor helmet was replaced with a clear "fishbowl" type for greater visibility, and the lunar surface EVA suit would include a water-cooled undergarment.\nImplications: 1. The Apollo program had multiple mission phases, including unmanned and manned flights.\n2. The first Block II CSM and LM were scheduled to be flown in a dual mission known as AS-207/208.\n3. The Block II crew positions had specific titles: Commander (CDR), Command Module Pilot (CMP), and Lunar Module Pilot (LMP).\n4. The astronauts would wear a new Apollo spacesuit, designed for lunar extravehicular activity (EVA).\n5. The traditional visor helmet was removed from the spacesuit and replaced with a clear "fishbowl" type for improved visibility.\n6. The lunar surface EVA suit would include a water-cooled undergarment for better protection and comfort during extravehicular activities.\n7. The Apollo program was planning to test and refine various components of the spacecraft before sending humans to the lunar surface.\n8. The program aimed to develop a reliable and safe lunar landing system to ensure a successful mission.\n9. The new Apollo spacesuit was designed to accommodate the demands of lunar EVA, indicating that the program was planning for more complex and comprehensive extravehicular activities.\n10. The clear "fishbowl" type helmet was a significant advancement in astronaut interface design, suggesting a commitment to improving astronauts\' ability to see and navigate in space.\nTopic: Apollo program\nHow many people were on the test flight of the AS-206?\nAnswer:\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=0, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[151643], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=64, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [10061, 594, 4226, 264, 3405, 5961, 323, 3529, 285, 974, 624, 14582, 25, 9608, 25, 4636, 458, 87840, 46414, 1273, 10971, 5752, 12, 17, 15, 21, 11, 264, 13627, 1035, 11466, 279, 1156, 8362, 7946, 356, 9501, 323, 46414, 304, 264, 18579, 8954, 3881, 438, 5752, 12, 17, 15, 22, 14, 17, 15, 23, 11, 476, 5752, 12, 17, 22, 23, 320, 9547, 41540, 1035, 387, 11626, 389, 264, 8651, 49153, 12102, 6138, 576, 8362, 7946, 13627, 9892, 1033, 24849, 34138, 320, 6484, 49, 8, 7348, 13711, 44499, 320, 93158, 8, 323, 78095, 13711, 44499, 320, 43, 5781, 568, 576, 68422, 1035, 3161, 12233, 264, 501, 35150, 12621, 3083, 11, 6188, 311, 28276, 56711, 11482, 523, 71, 23632, 5702, 320, 36, 12820, 568, 576, 8606, 2098, 269, 31535, 572, 12575, 448, 264, 2797, 330, 18170, 65, 9605, 1, 943, 369, 7046, 23160, 11, 323, 279, 56711, 7329, 468, 12820, 7781, 1035, 2924, 264, 3015, 22471, 41731, 1212, 12164, 478, 624, 25216, 10709, 25, 220, 16, 13, 576, 35150, 2025, 1030, 5248, 8954, 34430, 11, 2670, 87840, 323, 88325, 24908, 624, 17, 13, 576, 1156, 8362, 7946, 356, 9501, 323, 46414, 1033, 13537, 311, 387, 52750, 304, 264, 18579, 8954, 3881, 438, 5752, 12, 17, 15, 22, 14, 17, 15, 23, 624, 18, 13, 576, 8362, 7946, 13627, 9892, 1030, 3151, 15311, 25, 34138, 320, 6484, 49, 701, 7348, 13711, 44499, 320, 93158, 701, 323, 78095, 13711, 44499, 320, 43, 5781, 4292, 19